            <ArticleTitle>Electrophysiologic and neuropsychologic evaluation of patients with centrotemporal spikes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>995-1008</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/00207450701768929</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The present study was designed to evaluate neurocognitive functions with endogenous potentials and neurophysiologic tests in patients with centrotemporal spikes who were not on any medication. Of the patients, 85.7% had seizures, 9.5% had pavor nocturnes, and 4.8% had atypical headache. The patients, especially who had atypical seizures or left-sided epileptic activity, were found to have significant visuomotor function impairment (p &lt;.05). In P300 test, N2P3 amplitude was lower in the patients, particularly who had left sided epileptic activity (p &lt;.05). MMN and LDN results were normal. Serial evaluations of such patients with endogenous potentials and neuropsychological tests may be helpful to show development of neurocognitive impairment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
